{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"result":{"tools":[{"name":"waggledance_projects","description":"List every registered project: id, name, root path, and when it was last seen.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{}}},{"name":"waggledance_bee_upgrade","description":"Upgrade bee in the registered projects that already use bee. Previews by default. With `apply: true` it runs `bee onboard --apply` and commits only bee's files. `apply` requires a non-empty `projects` list the human has approved.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"apply":{"type":"boolean","default":false,"description":"Run the upgrade and commit; false previews"},"projects":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"},"description":"Project ids to narrow the run to; required with apply"}}}},{"name":"waggledance_ask_state","description":"Ask waggledance for a project's parsed bee state (active feature, phase, open/blocked cells, recent decisions, sessions, handoff, attention) without reading any .bee file yourself. Omit `project` to get a rollup across every registered project; each rollup digest drops `sessions` and `recent_decisions` and carries `session_count` instead, while a one-project answer keeps every field. Two fields describe the fleet rather than the store: `herding` names the agent kinds this project offers — {default, agents, resolvable} as LABELS, never the argv behind them, and a label listed in `agents` but absent from `resolvable` is one that will not start today; `panes` is the project's own live pane inventory (pane_id, kind, status, bee_state, bee_feature, workspace, tab), which is how you find an idle pane to reuse instead of spawning a new one. `panes` is ABSENT when the terminal surface is switched off, `null` beside a `panes_error` reason when herdr cannot be read — the bee state is complete either way — and an array otherwise. Seeing a pane grants nothing: dispatch is refused in a project whose .waggledance.toml says `orchestration = false`. `leaders` lists the Paseo agents labelled waggledance.role=leader whose cwd sits in the project's root or one of its worktrees, oldest first: {agent_id, status, cwd, created, via, labels, waiting_on, last_daemon_run, runs}, where `via` is `wd` for a leader carrying waggledance.via=wd and `daemon` otherwise, and `runs` is the work that leader carries, newest first, at most 5: {run_id, request_id, status, follow_up_of, steer, task_summary}, `task_summary` being the task's first line that is not a spec-drop or wd provenance line or the steer marker, cut to 200 characters, and `steer` true for a context note sent with `steer: true` rather than work. Read `runs` before every leader dispatch, leaders included, skipping steer rows, and pick one of three tiers: (1) same work — same feature, same files, same output — dispatch with `follow_up_of` set to that run_id; (2) same cluster — same area or module or overlapping paths, different feature — open the new leader, then send the running leader a `steer: true` note naming the new request, its area, the new run_id and what to watch for; (3) unrelated — new leader, no steer. When a summary is not enough to judge, read the run's full `task` from `waggledance_runs` with its `run_id` argument. Each leader also carries `family_root` (the run id every run it carries descends from, null when it carries none) and `current` (true when it is the family's newest leader, the one a follow-up reaches). `leaders` is ABSENT when `[paseo] enabled = false`, `null` beside a `leaders_error` reason when Paseo cannot be read, and an array (possibly empty) beside `leaders_skipped`, the count of leaders under no registered project, otherwise.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"project":{"type":"string","description":"Optional project id to narrow to a single project's full snapshot"}}}},{"name":"waggledance_dispatch","description":"Dispatch a task to an agent pane in a managed project. Every project takes dispatch unless its .waggledance.toml says `orchestration = false`; pane dispatch also needs terminal.enabled, and a refusal names the remedy. Either spawn a fresh agent via an operator-configured preset label, target an already-running pane by id, or hand the task to the project's Paseo leader with `leader: true` (queued and delivered by the waggledance daemon once the leader is idle) — exactly one of `preset`/`pane_id`/`leader`, and never a raw command. With `leader: true`, `follow_up_of` names a run (its run_id or request_id) whose leader should take this task instead of a new leader: first read `waggledance_ask_state` leaders[].runs — every caller, leaders included — and pick a tier. Same work (same feature, same files, same output): pass that run_id here and no fresh correlation id in the task. Same cluster (same area or module or overlapping paths, different feature): open a new leader whose task names the running leader's run_id and task_summary, then dispatch again with `follow_up_of` set to the running run and `steer: true`, the task naming the new request, its area, the new run_id and what to watch for. Unrelated: leave `follow_up_of` out and a new leader opens. The daemon waits while that leader is mid-turn, sends to it once idle, and starts a new leader carrying the earlier task text when it is gone. `steer: true` puts a fixed context-only marker above the task; a steer whose leader is gone ends `lost` and opens no leader; it is refused without `follow_up_of` or with `handoff: true`. With `leader: true` and `follow_up_of`, `handoff: true` starts a fresh leader that takes over the family and retires the older one — use it when your own context is nearly full; `handoff` without `follow_up_of` is refused, `handoff: false` opts out of the automatic handoff a leader's own dispatch otherwise becomes — a leader opening a new leader for other work passes `handoff: false`. Returns a `run_id` immediately; poll completion with `waggledance_await`.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"project":{"type":"string","description":"Registered project id"},"preset":{"type":"string","description":"Operator-configured agent preset label to spawn a fresh pane — mutually exclusive with pane_id"},"pane_id":{"type":"string","description":"An already-running pane id to target — mutually exclusive with preset"},"leader":{"type":"boolean","description":"true sends the task to the project's Paseo leader — mutually exclusive with preset and pane_id"},"task":{"type":"string","description":"The task text sent to the agent"},"request_id":{"type":"string","description":"Caller-chosen id for this request — pass one on every call so a retry never sends twice"},"follow_up_of":{"type":"string","description":"With leader: true only — the run_id or request_id of a run in this project whose leader should take this task as a follow-up instead of a new leader"},"handoff":{"type":"boolean","description":"With leader: true and follow_up_of — true starts a fresh leader that takes over that run's family and retires the older leader; false opts out of the automatic handoff"},"steer":{"type":"boolean","description":"With leader: true and follow_up_of — true sends the task to that run's leader as a context-only note about nearby work, never as work to build; never with handoff: true"}},"required":["project","task"]}},{"name":"waggledance_await","description":"Block until a dispatched run completes, blocks on a human, or the timeout elapses — whichever comes first, at most 60 seconds regardless of the `timeout_seconds` requested (a longer request is silently clamped, never honored, never an error). Returns `status` (working/done/blocked/timeout) and the transcript `delta` since dispatch.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"run_id":{"type":"string","description":"The run_id returned by waggledance_dispatch"},"timeout_seconds":{"type":"integer","description":"Max seconds to wait, clamped to 60 server-side (default 60)"}},"required":["run_id"]}},{"name":"waggledance_runs","description":"List dispatched runs and their current status (read-only, D8), optionally filtered to one project. Each row carries `task_summary`, the task's first line that is not a spec-drop or wd provenance line or the steer marker, cut to 200 characters, never the full `task`. Pass `run_id` to get exactly that run as a one-row list carrying its full `task` beside `task_summary`; an unknown id is a tool error.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"project":{"type":"string","description":"Optional project id to narrow to a single project's runs"},"run_id":{"type":"string","description":"Optional run id: return only that run, with its full task"}}}},{"name":"waggledance_task","description":"Follow up on a task wd handed to a project's Paseo leader, by its corr. `reply` and `redo` queue the user's words for that leader; `accept` closes the task and archives its leader when nothing else uses it; `allow` and `deny` answer the leader's permission request `req_id` (the newest unanswered one when absent). Only ever pass the user's own allow or deny.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"corr":{"type":"string","description":"The task's correlation id"},"action":{"type":"string","enum":["reply","redo","accept","allow","deny"]},"text":{"type":"string","description":"The user's words — required for reply and redo"},"req_id":{"type":"string","description":"The permission request to answer — allow and deny only"}},"required":["corr","action"]}}]}}
